  The same client, under the two orders:

  | auth two-step order | MAC D's EAPOL frames | 802.1X reached?                 | final state         | dataplane |
  | ------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------- | ------------------- | --------- |
  | auth-mac dot1x      | 1                    | no, no Identity request at all  | Unauthorized, Held  | 0 of 4    |
  | dot1x auth-mac      | 8                    | yes, and it succeeds at 1.009 s | Unauthorized, Held  | 0 of 4    |

  => STEP 3 PASS, with two results in one leg.  First, the order keyword decides which
     method is tried first, and the wire shows it with nothing else changed: 1 frame
     against 8, for the same MAC with the same credentials.  Second, both steps are
     really required, in either order.  With `dot1x auth-mac`, MAC D passed 802.1X
     outright at 1.009 s and is still Unauthorized at 0 of 4, because the MAC step then
     failed.  Read together with STEP 1c, where the client passed MAC and failed 802.1X
     and was also Unauthorized, this proves that either step failing blocks
     authorisation, whichever one is configured first.

  One wire detail, recorded as seen: in the reversed order the DUT sent
  `EAP-Request/Identity id=1` twice, 6 milliseconds apart, and the client answered both.
  This did not happen in any `auth-mac dot1x` leg.  The exchange continued normally to
  EAP-Success.  It is noted only so that the count of 8 is not read as 6 plus a problem.

