     came from a console tool's own cleanup, not from a case step.

STEP 5 -- `auth-mac enable` deletes `dot1x control-direction both` in silence  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: before this case the port carried `dot1x control-direction both`.  One command
  was sent and the running-config was read straight after.

awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)# auth-mac enable
awplus(config-if)# end

awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.48
!
interface port1.0.48
 switchport
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 48
 auth-mac enable
 dot1x port-control auto          <-- the control-direction line has gone
!
awplus# show auth interface port1.0.48 | include CD
  CD: adminControlledDirections: in

  No warning was printed, no `% ` line, and no log entry was made.  Now the same two
  commands in the other order:

awplus(config-if)# dot1x control-direction both
% port1.0.48: DOT1X control-direction cannot be modified on a Web-auth or Mac-auth enabled port

  => STEP 5 PASS, and this is a finding.  The two settings cannot both be in force, and
     the product knows it: there is a clear and specific error message for exactly this
     conflict.  But the message is printed only when the operator sets
     `control-direction` after `auth-mac`.  In the other order, which is the natural one
     because an operator secures the port first and adds MAC authentication later, the
     existing setting is deleted without a word.  An operator who chose `both` on purpose
     is never told it has gone.  To put it back, `no auth-mac enable` must come first.

